[23:45:20] [PROCESS] The boundary test computes a sample-size-weighted mean H0 for massive hosts at z > 0.25 and compares it to the low-z (z < 0.15) value. The calibration-independent relative change delta_H0 = H0(high-z) - H0(low-z) is the primary diagnostic: the KBC gradual decay predicts a decline, while TEP predicts a flat or increasing profile. The absolute H0 at high z is not an independent measurement because Pantheon+ MU_SH0ES inherits the Cepheid zero-point by construction.
[23:45:20] [PROCESS] Testing void boundary prediction...
[23:45:20] [TEST]   Massive hosts at z > 0.25: H0 = 73.5 +/- 0.38 km/s/Mpc (4 bins)
[23:45:20] [TEST]   Tension with CMB: 9.7 sigma (H0 - H0_CMB = 6.1) [NOT calibration-independent]
[23:45:20] [TEST]   Low-mass hosts at z > 0.25: H0 = 74.4 km/s/Mpc
[23:45:20] [TEST]   Massive hosts at z < 0.15: H0 = 72.3 km/s/Mpc
[23:45:20] [TEST]   Verdict: KBC gradual decay NOT observed: H0 changes from 72.3 at z < 0.15 to 73.5 at z > 0.25 (Δ = +1.2 km/s/Mpc). The published KBC/MOND model (HBK20/Mazurenko et al. 2024) predicts a gradual decline towards the Planck value (67.4), but the observed H0 does not decline — it is flat or increases. NOTE: The absolute H0 = 73.5 at z > 0.25 is NOT an independent measurement; Pantheon+ MU_SH0ES uses M_B = -19.253 calibrated from Cepheid anchors, so the Cepheid zero-point is inherited by construction. The calibration-independent test is the relative evolution, which rejects the KBC gradual decline. The TEP prediction (flat H0(z) for global M_B) is consistent with the observed flat profile. The host-mass dependence of the TEP effect is established in TEP-H0 (Paper 11) using per-host Cepheid distances, not the global M_B used here.
[23:45:20] [TEST] Boundary test verdict: void_falsified=True, tep_supported=True. The calibration-independent relative evolution determines whether the KBC gradual decline is observed or whether the TEP flat prediction is favoured.
[23:45:20] [PROCESS] Model comparison uses AIC with both fitted (k=2) and fixed-prediction (k=1) parameterizations. Void models (Gaussian sigma_z and Exponential z_0) are fit with free dH0 and shape parameter; the TEP model is fit with free dH0 and decay index n. Fixed-prediction comparisons use published void parameters (sigma_z=0.82, z_0=0.74) against the TEP flat H0(z) prediction for global M_B. Delta AIC > 10 is considered decisive preference for TEP.
